From: Kun Zhang <zhangkun2@uniontech.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 16:39:32 +0800
Subject: QQuickFlickable: keep using pixelDelta on touchpads with rounded deltas

Scrolling a Flickable slowly with a two-finger touchpad gesture stutters:
the content stops following the fingers, jerks ahead of them and springs
back, and when the fingers are lifted it keeps travelling on its own.
Scrolling at normal speed is fine, and the slower the gesture, the worse
it gets.

QQuickFlickable::wheelEvent falls back to the angleDelta (mouse wheel)
code path whenever QWheelEvent::pixelDelta() is null.  That test inspects
one event, not the device, so it is also met in the middle of a touchpad
gesture: while the fingers move slowly they travel less than one pixel
between two frames, so the Wayland QPA rounds that motion down to (0, 0)
in QWaylandInputDevice::Pointer::FrameData::pixelDeltaAndError() and carries
the remainder over to the next frame, while angleDelta stays non-null because
it is derived from the same continuous motion.

Those updates then feed the velocity buffer and call flickX()/flickY()
while the drag started by ScrollBegin is still in progress, so a flick
animation and the remaining drag updates write to contentX/contentY at
once.  In a captured trace of one slow scroll, 13 of 98 ScrollUpdate
frames were affected, the first one as early as the third update.

Keep such an update on the pixelDelta path instead.  Dragging by its
(0, 0) delta holds the content still until the accumulated rounding error
produces the next whole pixel, which is exactly what the fingers did.

The three existing conditions are unchanged, so only touchpad updates
that have an angleDelta but no pixelDelta change behaviour.  Mouse wheels
keep taking the angleDelta path: their events are MouseEventNotSynthesized
and their phase is NoScrollPhase, and either term alone is enough.  Mouse
press-drag-release does not go through wheelEvent() at all.

Bug: https://bugreports.qt.io/browse/QTBUG-149567
Forwarded: https://codereview.qt-project.org/c/qt/qtdeclarative/+/764706
---
 src/quick/items/qquickflickable.cpp | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/quick/items/qquickflickable.cpp b/src/quick/items/qquickflickable.cpp
index b5ab2ab7a..136a5eebb 100644
--- a/src/quick/items/qquickflickable.cpp
+++ b/src/quick/items/qquickflickable.cpp
@@ -1661,7 +1661,15 @@ void QQuickFlickable::wheelEvent(QWheelEvent *event)
         return;
     }
 
-    if (event->source() == Qt::MouseEventNotSynthesized || event->pixelDelta().isNull() || event->phase() == Qt::NoScrollPhase) {
+    // Within a touchpad gesture, pixelDelta rounds down to (0, 0) whenever the fingers
+    // moved less than one pixel since the previous frame. Keep such a frame on the
+    // pixelDelta path: falling back to angleDelta would start a wheel flick in the
+    // middle of the ongoing drag.
+    const bool isTouchpadFrameWithLostPixelDelta =
+            event->deviceType() == QInputDevice::DeviceType::TouchPad
+            && !event->angleDelta().isNull();
+    if (event->source() == Qt::MouseEventNotSynthesized || event->phase() == Qt::NoScrollPhase
+        || (event->pixelDelta().isNull() && !isTouchpadFrameWithLostPixelDelta)) {
         // no pixel delta (physical mouse wheel, or "dumb" touchpad), so use angleDelta
         int xDelta = event->angleDelta().x();
         int yDelta = event->angleDelta().y();
